  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Fog likely the next two nights, especially \nDowneast. Fog could be dense enough to impact travel especially \nnear the coast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nSkies this (Tuesday) afternoon remain mostly clear, with warm \ntemperatures in the mid 70s to mid 80s. However, a southerly \nbreeze is beginning to bring increased low level moisture. This \nlow level moisture off the Gulf of Maine will increase in \nearnest tonight as the threat of fog develops. Low clouds and \nfog move to the Downeast coast late this afternoon/early this \nevening, with the low clouds gradually progressing north/inland \nthrough the night. Think that for inland areas, it will mostly \nbe low clouds and not as much visibility-reducing fog. However, \nfor areas near the coast, the chance of impactful fog with lower\nvisibilities is higher. \n\nDuring the day Wednesday, some fog could persist right along \nthe coast, but everywhere else should see lifting clouds. \nHowever, the threat of fog returns again Wednesday evening, \nwith again the greater threat being near the coast, though fog \nis possible anywhere. Other than patchy light drizzle, rain \nDowneast from an approaching system from the south appears to \nhold off until very late Wednesday night or Thursday morning. \nSee more in key message 2 below.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain late Wednesday into Thursday night, with\nhigher totals Downeast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...Low pressure system slows over the \nNortheast, eventually moving east late week. It tracks through\nthe northeast, before going into the Gulf of Maine Thursday\nnight into Friday. Wetting rainfall expected, with higher \ntotals along Downeast, as early as Wednesday night or Thursday. \nRainfall for Downeast seems to be trending down, so around 1/2 \nan inch of accumulation is likely. There is some potential for \na thunderstorm Thursday afternoon, but not anticipating severe\nconditions. Rainfall moves out by Friday morning.",
